WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.333 --> 00:00:02.368
-How are you, Michael?

00:00:02.368 --> 00:00:03.402
-Great.
Thanks for having me.

00:00:03.402 --> 00:00:04.403
-Thank you so much
for being here.

00:00:04.403 --> 00:00:05.472
So I was fascinated

00:00:05.472 --> 00:00:07.807
by the contents of this book,
but also,

00:00:07.807 --> 00:00:11.610
just what goes into covering
stories like these?

00:00:11.610 --> 00:00:14.414
The Comey firing,
the Mueller investigation.

00:00:14.414 --> 00:00:16.849
You met sources at airports
late at night.

00:00:16.849 --> 00:00:18.851
You had to chase them down
in D.C.

00:00:18.851 --> 00:00:22.288
It must've been
quite an undertaking.

00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:26.659
-I mean, covering a Trump
story was like another --

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:29.361
like nothing we had
ever covered before,

00:00:29.361 --> 00:00:31.931
primarily because of the speed.

00:00:31.931 --> 00:00:33.432
It was just so fast

00:00:33.432 --> 00:00:36.102
and the news cycles
turned over so quickly.

00:00:36.102 --> 00:00:37.970
And we went to great lengths,
as you were saying.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:39.171
I lay out in the book

00:00:39.171 --> 00:00:41.507
about getting up
at 3:00 in the morning

00:00:41.507 --> 00:00:45.277
and driving to airports
to pick people up

00:00:45.277 --> 00:00:47.613
that were flying in,
who I wanted to talk to,

00:00:47.613 --> 00:00:49.782
thinking that was the only time

00:00:49.782 --> 00:00:53.653
I would get a chance
to actually speak with them.

00:00:53.653 --> 00:00:57.389
And to stay competitive
and in this story,

00:00:57.389 --> 00:01:00.559
we pushed ourselves
in ways that I think

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:03.295
we never thought
we'd be tested in.

00:01:03.295 --> 00:01:06.132
-You have a brilliant
piece of wording

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:09.836
in describing what it's like
to interview Donald Trump,

00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:13.305
which is you say it's like
"unzipping fog,"

00:01:13.305 --> 00:01:15.574
[ Laughing ]
which will stay with me
a long time.

00:01:15.574 --> 00:01:18.143
You did manage to sit down
and interview him

00:01:18.143 --> 00:01:21.380
at Mar-a-Lago,
but it wasn't really scheduled.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:23.582
How did it come about?

00:01:23.582 --> 00:01:26.853
-So, what I was able to do
was I was able to have one

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:29.855
of the president's friends
take me to lunch at his club

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:32.858
and the friend basically said,
"When the president comes

00:01:32.858 --> 00:01:34.894
off the course,
we'll go over and talk to him

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:36.495
and maybe you
can interview him."

00:01:36.495 --> 00:01:38.965
So, we did this.
I was there.

00:01:38.965 --> 00:01:41.033
I looked like I was wearing
golf clothes.

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:43.736
And we went over
and the president greeted me

00:01:43.736 --> 00:01:47.574
and I crouched down next
to him like a catcher

00:01:47.574 --> 00:01:49.508
because I wanted
his undivided attention

00:01:49.508 --> 00:01:51.443
and I knew,
if I was just standing there

00:01:51.443 --> 00:01:53.413
at the table with other people,

00:01:53.413 --> 00:01:55.248
then I wouldn't get a chance
to talk to him

00:01:55.248 --> 00:01:57.183
and I needed to sort of
convince him

00:01:57.183 --> 00:01:59.352
that he would sit with me
for an interview.

00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:01.454
So I crouched like a catcher

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:03.656
and I sat there
as long as I could

00:02:03.656 --> 00:02:04.924
and he was going on and on

00:02:04.924 --> 00:02:07.860
about state and local
taxes and stuff.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:09.529
And I said, "Mr. President" --

00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:11.430
My legs were killing me

00:02:11.430 --> 00:02:15.501
and I said, "Mr. President,
this is all really interesting.

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:17.169
Why don't we sit down
and do an interview?"

00:02:17.169 --> 00:02:19.939
And he said, "Okay,
let me just finish lunch,"

00:02:19.939 --> 00:02:22.442
which I describe as him
eating a head of lettuce

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:23.610
that looked like it
had been dowsed

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:25.278
in Thousand Island dressing.
-[ Laughing ]

00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:29.916
-So I go back to my seat and,
within a matter of moments,

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:32.552
I hear, "Michael, Michael,
get over here!"

00:02:32.552 --> 00:02:34.220
And I had a chance to sit
and interview

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:37.389
the president
for a half an hour.

00:02:37.389 --> 00:02:40.559
-It's really amazing,
the energy he has for talking

00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:42.061
when he's had his lettuce.

00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:43.662
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.662 --> 00:02:45.798
You draw attention to --
you know, I think people

00:02:45.798 --> 00:02:48.134
who follow the story
have heard the name Don McGahn,

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:49.835
but maybe don't know him as well

00:02:49.835 --> 00:02:51.704
as they, say,
know a Comey or Mueller.

00:02:51.704 --> 00:02:53.372
You describe him as one
of the most fascinating

00:02:53.372 --> 00:02:54.940
people of this story.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.443
Can you talk about how you
came to that conclusion,

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:59.879
insofar as the role
Don McGahn has played

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:02.481
in the Trump administration?

00:03:02.481 --> 00:03:04.016
-So, McGahn, to me,

00:03:04.016 --> 00:03:06.418
is consequential
for so many reasons.

00:03:06.418 --> 00:03:10.189
First of all, he was in charge
of the umbilical cord,

00:03:10.189 --> 00:03:12.825
the tie between Trump and his
base, which is the judges,

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:14.960
the thing that allowed
conservatives

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.496
to stay with the president
when he behaved in ways

00:03:17.496 --> 00:03:20.833
that they found to be unsavory.

00:03:20.833 --> 00:03:23.302
Because of that and because
of the impact McGahn had,

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:26.005
we will be living
in Don McGahn's America

00:03:26.005 --> 00:03:27.640
for decades and decades to come,

00:03:27.640 --> 00:03:29.842
because of how he remade
the federal judiciary.

00:03:29.842 --> 00:03:31.944
He was also a chief
container of Trump,

00:03:31.944 --> 00:03:33.947
someone who was stopping
the president

00:03:33.947 --> 00:03:35.982
from hurting himself
and the country

00:03:35.982 --> 00:03:37.883
and the office
of the presidency.

00:03:37.883 --> 00:03:41.854
And, on top of that, he was
a witness against the president

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:43.957
in an existential threat
to the presidency,

00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:45.558
being the Mueller investigation.

00:03:45.558 --> 00:03:47.493
So McGahn, as a lawyer,

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:50.529
was being used as a witness
against his client.

00:03:50.529 --> 00:03:52.398
Now, the question you probably
may have is like,

00:03:52.398 --> 00:03:55.134
"Why would McGahn stay,
if this was all going on?"

00:03:55.134 --> 00:03:58.271
He stayed because he had
a never and again opportunity

00:03:58.271 --> 00:04:01.640
to remake the courts and was
willing to endure all of this

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:06.378
and the risks of it, in order
to get the courts remade.

00:04:06.378 --> 00:04:09.415
-There is this idea
that you talk about

00:04:09.415 --> 00:04:12.218
and, obviously, others
have talked about, you know,

00:04:12.218 --> 00:04:15.622
especially when we talk
about why did people stay,

00:04:15.622 --> 00:04:18.958
which is the idea
of adults in the room.

00:04:18.958 --> 00:04:21.794
Do you feel like this
theory, in the end,

00:04:21.794 --> 00:04:23.128
turned out to be a bad one,

00:04:23.128 --> 00:04:25.564
that they were not particularly
good adults in the room,

00:04:25.564 --> 00:04:27.366
or was it just a reminder
that some adults

00:04:27.366 --> 00:04:29.868
cannot control their children?

00:04:29.868 --> 00:04:33.639
-I think that there were
more adults earlier

00:04:33.639 --> 00:04:37.410
in the administration
and they had more success.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.847
That does not mean that they
had complete success

00:04:40.847 --> 00:04:42.848
or should be absolved
for anything that happened

00:04:42.848 --> 00:04:44.783
in the first few years
of the administration.

00:04:44.783 --> 00:04:47.853
But the John Kellys
of the world, the Don McGahns,

00:04:47.853 --> 00:04:50.856
were more of a barrier
around the president.

00:04:50.856 --> 00:04:54.060
The president effectively
got rid of people like that

00:04:54.060 --> 00:04:58.898
and has less barriers
in his way now.

00:04:58.898 --> 00:05:01.400
And I think that has left
the president

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:04.470
even more emboldened
than ever before

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:07.473
and he has a very unusual
use of power

00:05:07.473 --> 00:05:09.175
and interest in using power

00:05:09.175 --> 00:05:13.613
and there are those fewer
guardrails now around him.

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:17.216
-Well, Michael, I'm so impressed
that you wrote this book

00:05:17.216 --> 00:05:20.053
[ Laughing ]
while you continue
to be a working reporter.

00:05:20.053 --> 00:05:21.854
I do not know how
you juggle them both,

00:05:21.854 --> 00:05:24.657
but I am happy on each count

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:27.393
and thank you so much
for being with us tonight.

00:05:27.393 --> 00:05:28.728
-Thanks so much for having me.

